---
import ArticleLayout from "../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro";

const title = "AI Code Review False Positives: Fix the Gate";
const description =
  "Handle AI code review false positives with evidence, explicit dispositions, and a clear separation between comment filters and merge blockers.";
const canonical = "https://reviewgate.lvtd.dev/blog/ai-code-review-false-positives/";
const publishedAt = "2026-09-10";
const steps = [
  { name: "Recover the exact finding", text: "Start with the current reviewed commit, stable finding identity, claim, and repository evidence.", anchor: "recover" },
  { name: "Classify the disagreement", text: "Separate a disproved defect from optional advice, accepted risk, missing context, and a stale result.", anchor: "classify" },
  { name: "Inspect the blocking policy", text: "Distinguish severity and comment visibility from evidence admission and the canonical blocking reason.", anchor: "policy" },
  { name: "Record a bounded disposition", text: "Attach evidence to the finding and state what future change would invalidate the decision.", anchor: "disposition" },
  { name: "Change the smallest responsible rule", text: "Use the observed root cause to improve context or review instructions without excluding unrelated defects.", anchor: "tune" },
];
const faqs = [
  { question: "Does a high-confidence AI finding have to block a pull request?", answer: "No. In ReviewGate, confidence is one input to finding admission. Eligible classification and severity, repository-evidence validation, and the applicable verification and convergence rules also matter. Use the canonical blocking reason and disposition rather than severity or confident prose alone." },
  { question: "Does min_severity reduce false-positive blockers?", answer: "Not by itself. ReviewGate uses min_severity to filter inline publication, not to change score computation. A lower-severity validated blocker can still affect the score when its inline comment is hidden. Diagnose the finding's evidence and canonical disposition instead." },
  { question: "Is an accepted risk a false positive?", answer: "No. Accepting a risk means acknowledging a real issue and deciding how to handle it. A false positive means the alleged defect does not hold under the applicable code and contract. Keep those decisions separate so later maintainers can understand what was actually established." },
];
const jsonLd = [
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org", "@type": "BlogPosting",
    headline: title, description, datePublished: publishedAt, dateModified: publishedAt,
    mainEntityOfPage: canonical,
    author: { "@type": "Organization", name: "ReviewGate maintainers", url: "https://github.com/LVTD-LLC/reviewgate" },
    publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "LVTD LLC", url: "https://lvtd.dev" },
  },
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org", "@type": "HowTo",
    name: "How to handle a suspected AI code review false positive",
    description: "An evidence-backed workflow for a contested finding and its merge-blocking state.",
    step: steps.map((step, index) => ({ "@type": "HowToStep", position: index + 1, name: step.name, text: step.text, url: `${canonical}#${step.anchor}` })),
  },
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org", "@type": "FAQPage",
    mainEntity: faqs.map((faq) => ({ "@type": "Question", name: faq.question, acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: faq.answer } })),
  },
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org", "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
    itemListElement: [
      { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "ReviewGate", item: "https://reviewgate.lvtd.dev/" },
      { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Blog", item: "https://reviewgate.lvtd.dev/blog/" },
      { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: title, item: canonical },
    ],
  },
];
---

<ArticleLayout title={title} description={description} publishedAt={publishedAt} readingTime="10 min read" jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <Fragment slot="title">AI code review false positives: fix the gate.</Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="deck">
    A disputed finding needs a decision backed by code. Hiding its comment, lowering
    its severity, or asking the model again does not establish that the finding is wrong.
  </Fragment>
  <ol slot="toc">
    <li><a href="#short-answer">Short answer</a></li>
    <li><a href="#recover">1. Recover the exact finding</a></li>
    <li><a href="#classify">2. Classify the disagreement</a></li>
    <li><a href="#policy">3. Inspect the blocking policy</a></li>
    <li><a href="#disposition">4. Record a bounded disposition</a></li>
    <li><a href="#tune">5. Change the responsible rule</a></li>
    <li><a href="#example">Worked example</a></li>
    <li><a href="#mistakes">Mistakes and trade-offs</a></li>
    <li><a href="#faq">Questions</a></li>
  </ol>

  <section id="short-answer">
    <h2>How should you handle AI code review false positives?</h2>
    <p class="article-lead">
      Treat a suspected AI code review false positive as a disputed claim, not a command
      to rewrite working code. Recover the exact reviewed commit, inspect the claimed
      failure path, separate advice from defects, and record a disposition with evidence.
      Change the reviewer only after you know why its claim failed.
    </p>
    <p>
      This tutorial is for a maintainer facing one noisy or contested pull-request
      review. You need access to the diff, relevant repository contracts, and the review
      result. The examples use ReviewGate's documented behavior as of September 10, 2026.
      They are not a benchmark or a claim that ReviewGate eliminates incorrect findings.
    </p>
    <ol>
      {steps.map((step) => <li><strong>{step.name}.</strong> {step.text}</li>)}
    </ol>
    <p>
      AI reviewers can flag nonexistent problems, misunderstand code, or suggest an
      inaccurate fix. GitHub documents those limitations in its
      <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/responsible-use/agents#7-limitations">Copilot Agents application card</a>.
      The practical response is to keep finding generation separate from the decision
      that a finding must stop a merge. A reviewer can be useful without being granted
      the final word on every allegation.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="recover">
    <h2>1. Recover the exact finding before changing code</h2>
    <p>
      Start with the result for the code you are actually deciding about. An old comment
      may describe a removed branch, a renamed function, or a guard that was added after
      the review. That makes the comment stale; it does not tell you whether the original
      finding was right. Preserve that distinction when recording the outcome.
    </p>
    <p>
      For ReviewGate, use the stable <code>.reviewgate/result.json</code> contract for
      automation and the full <code>.reviewgate/review.json</code> artifact for deeper
      diagnostics. Follow the <a href="/docs/artifacts">artifact retrieval guide</a> to
      obtain a result bound to the repository, pull request, and current
      <code>reviewed_sha</code>. Do not build a repair queue by scraping comment prose.
    </p>
    <p>
      Once you have that current, validated artifact, this documented filter selects the
      canonical score-blocking work. It is an inspection example, not a complete merge
      authorization script:
    </p>
    <pre><code>{`jq '
  [.findings[]
   | select(.disposition == "still_open"
            and .blocking_reason != null)]
' .reviewgate/result.json`}</code></pre>
    <p>
      Keep the <code>semantic_fingerprint</code> with each selected finding. It identifies
      the finding for dispositions even when its wording or line anchor changes. Read
      the claim and evidence together: what input reaches the changed code, which
      invariant fails, and what observable consequence follows? A label such as
      “possible race condition” does not answer those questions.
    </p>
    <p>
      This step is done when you can point to one current claim and its supporting code.
      If the result is <code>review_error</code> with a null score, stop treating it as a
      verdict on the diff. The reviewer did not produce a usable completed result;
      investigate the typed error through the documented retry path.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="classify">
    <h2>2. Classify the disagreement, not just the comment</h2>
    <p>
      Reserve “false positive” for an allegation that does not hold. A correct defect
      that the team postpones remains a correct defect. A helpful suggestion that should
      never have blocked the merge is a policy or classification problem, even if its
      advice is sound. Separating those cases gives you a useful next action.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>What you establish</th><th>Decision</th><th>Useful next action</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>The claimed failure is reachable and violates the contract</td><td>Real defect</td><td>Repair it or explicitly route the risk decision to its owner</td></tr>
        <tr><td>A guard or contract disproves the alleged failure</td><td>False positive</td><td>Record the contradicting evidence against this finding</td></tr>
        <tr><td>The suggestion improves code without correcting a defect</td><td>Advice</td><td>Keep it nonblocking; decide whether it belongs in this PR</td></tr>
        <tr><td>The relevant business rule is undecided</td><td>Contract ambiguity</td><td>Ask the responsible owner to settle the rule</td></tr>
        <tr><td>The claim was correct on an earlier head and has been repaired</td><td>Fixed finding</td><td>Record the repair and current-head resolution evidence</td></tr>
        <tr><td>A real issue is knowingly retained</td><td>Accepted risk</td><td>Record ownership, rationale, and the follow-up boundary</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      These are triage categories for this workflow, not a new ReviewGate status enum.
      <a href="https://docs.semgrep.dev/for-developers/resolve-findings-through-app">Semgrep's triage documentation</a>
      makes a similar important distinction by offering separate reasons for a false
      positive, acceptable risk, and lack of time to fix. Do not use the first label to
      conceal the other two decisions.
    </p>
    <p>
      Optional feedback also deserves an explicit label.
      <a href="https://google.github.io/eng-practices/review/reviewer/comments.html">Google's code-review guidance</a>
      distinguishes required changes from optional comments so authors do not mistake
      every remark for a condition of approval. Our
      <a href="/blog/pull-request-review-comments">signal-first comment guide</a> covers
      wording and placement; the job here is deciding what the feedback means for the gate.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="policy">
    <h2>3. Diagnose AI code review false positives at the policy layer</h2>
    <p>
      ReviewGate does not derive a blocker from severity alone. Its
      <a href="https://github.com/LVTD-LLC/reviewgate/blob/87aa439/docs/finding-policy.md">published finding policy</a>
      separates classification, severity, confidence, evidence, and blocking disposition.
      An initial candidate needs an eligible classification, severity from P0 through P3,
      confidence of at least 0.85, and a passed repository-evidence gate before it can
      receive a deterministic blocking reason.
    </p>
    <p>
      Eligible classifications are <code>defect</code>, <code>security</code>, and
      <code>reliability_risk</code>. Suggestions, contract ambiguity, and P4 findings are
      advisory. The confidence threshold is a policy input; do not read a model's
      <code>0.85</code> as a demonstrated 85% probability that the defect exists.
      ReviewGate's <a href="/docs/features">features and scoring documentation</a>
      describes additional requirements for late blockers, verification, and convergence.
    </p>
    <p>
      The implementation makes the boundary concrete. In
      <a href="https://github.com/LVTD-LLC/reviewgate/blob/87aa439/crates/reviewgate-core/src/lib.rs">the core finding policy</a>,
      <code>expected_blocking_reason</code> considers eligibility, evidence-gate success,
      and any verifier decision. A different helper,
      <code>is_inline_comment_eligible</code>, considers publication eligibility and the
      configured severity floor. Those are different decisions, not two names for one setting.
    </p>
    <p>
      Consequently, setting <code>min_severity: P2</code> can hide a P3 inline comment
      without removing a validated P3 blocker from the score. If the finding is wrong,
      fix its evidence-backed disposition. If it is right but low priority, make an
      explicit policy decision. A quieter comment stream is not proof of a better verdict.
    </p>
    <p>
      This step is done when you know whether the problem is a false factual claim,
      incorrect classification, missing context, or merely unwanted publication. Change
      the control that owns that problem. Otherwise you can make the symptom disappear
      while leaving the actual blocking state unchanged.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="disposition">
    <h2>4. Record the decision and its limits</h2>
    <p>
      Use a compact record for contested findings. The six fields below are an editorial
      worksheet, not a payload to submit to ReviewGate. They connect the maintainer's
      reasoning to the product's existing artifact and disposition contracts.
    </p>
    <pre><code>{`Reviewed head: <exact commit>
Finding: <semantic_fingerprint>
Claim: <one falsifiable statement>
Evidence: <paths, lines, excerpts, and causal explanation>
Decision: <disproved, fixed, intentional, or unresolved>
Revisit when: <specific change that invalidates this evidence>`}</code></pre>
    <p>
      The last field prevents a local exception from becoming a permanent exemption.
      A guard can disprove a claim today and disappear tomorrow. State that dependency
      rather than adding “never flag this function” to a prompt. Preserve uncertainty
      where the evidence is incomplete: “I cannot reproduce it” is not equivalent to
      “this execution path cannot occur.”
    </p>
    <p>
      Submit the actual disposition using the
      <a href="/docs/artifacts#submit-a-disposition-without-hand-writing-json">documented CLI interface</a>,
      targeting the semantic fingerprint and supplying evidence. ReviewGate distinguishes
      submitted statuses from canonical tracked states. Use the documented enum rather
      than copying the worksheet's shorthand into an API payload.
    </p>
    <p>
      A resolved GitHub thread is also not the same as a resolved finding. The stable
      artifact can show <code>resolved_externally</code> while canonical state remains
      open or disputed. Treat that mismatch as work to reconcile, not permission to
      remove the obligation from an agent's task list. This step ends with a recorded,
      evidence-backed decision attached to the correct finding and head.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="tune">
    <h2>5. Change the smallest rule responsible for the mistake</h2>
    <p>
      Group repeated mistakes by the reason they happened. In his April 2026
      <a href="https://tech.bdigitalmedia.io/blog/diagnosing-ai-review-false-positives/">false-positive diagnosis article</a>,
      Bob Ulrich distinguishes clustering by root cause from clustering by the symptom
      a reviewer reported. “Missing validation” is a symptom. “The reviewer never
      inspected the upstream validator” describes a repairable context failure.
    </p>
    <p>
      For that failure, name the validator and require the review to trace its callers.
      For a mistaken business rule, supply the accepted contract. For a style preference
      promoted to a defect, clarify the classification boundary. Do not respond to one
      mistaken comment in a test file by excluding every test: a broken fixture can
      still hide a regression, and a helper can still encode the wrong assumption.
    </p>
    <p>
      ReviewGate also supports optional independent blocker verification. If you want
      that additional model call, merge this setting into your existing
      <code>.reviewgate.yml</code>:
    </p>
    <pre><code>{`verify_blockers: true`}</code></pre>
    <p>
      As documented in <a href="/docs/configuration#independently-verify-blocker-candidates">blocker verification configuration</a>,
      grounded candidates are checked together in at most one extra call. The verifier
      receives normalized claims and checked evidence, not the discovery model's
      persuasive title, detail, or repair instruction. Rejected candidates remain
      auditable in the full artifact; an inconclusive result becomes
      <code>review_error</code>, not a clean review.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is another model judgment, not an accuracy guarantee. A later rejection does
      not silently erase an already verified open obligation: the disagreement is
      retained until convergence accepts resolution evidence. That distinction matters
      when a team repeatedly reruns review hoping a stubborn finding will disappear.
      Use evidence to settle the disagreement instead of treating stochastic agreement
      as proof.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="example">
    <h2>Worked example: an alleged duplicate notification</h2>
    <p>
      Consider an illustrative PR that moves notification delivery into a background
      worker. The AI reviewer claims a retry sends the same notification twice. This is
      a hypothetical triage example, not a reported ReviewGate result or an executed test.
      Its purpose is to show how the same suspicious line can lead to different honest decisions.
    </p>
    <p>
      First, recover the worker code and exact reviewed head. Locate the retry entry
      point, the delivery operation, and any deduplication state. Do not assume that a
      function named <code>send_once</code> enforces the name's promise. Read how its key
      is constructed, where the state lives, and when it is written relative to delivery.
    </p>
    <p>
      Suppose the finding overlooked a shared delivery service. If that service enforces
      the relevant deduplication contract across the entire retry path, its implementation
      can contradict the allegation. Record the service and caller evidence, identify
      the exact claim it disproves, and submit an evidence-backed rejection. The worksheet's
      revisit condition should mention changes to that service, its key, or the call path.
    </p>
    <p>
      Now change one fact: the worker writes its “sent” marker only after delivery returns.
      A crash between those operations may leave a retry path that sends again. The
      presence of a marker is no longer enough to reject the finding. Establish whether
      another mechanism covers that window. If not, the alleged failure needs repair or
      an explicit product decision about duplicate delivery. Calling it a false positive
      because duplication is rare would record the wrong conclusion.
    </p>
    <p>
      Finally, suppose the intended product behavior is unclear. The maintainer must
      settle whether duplicate delivery is forbidden, tolerated, or handled elsewhere.
      Keep that question visible. Do not let the reviewer invent a stricter contract,
      and do not let an author invent an exception solely to close the thread. The
      useful outcome is a narrower, documented decision that the next review can reuse.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="mistakes">
    <h2>Common mistakes: optimizing for silence</h2>
    <p>
      Fewer false positives is a useful goal only alongside retained defect detection.
      A reviewer that emits nothing produces no false alarms and offers no review value.
      The strongest argument for tolerating some noise is that investigating an uncertain
      warning may be cheaper than missing a serious defect. Handle that trade-off by
      routing uncertain findings appropriately, not by insisting that every concern must
      either block automatically or disappear.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Do not lower severity to disprove a claim.</strong> Impact and correctness are separate questions.</li>
      <li><strong>Do not count every rejection as a model error.</strong> Fixed findings, changed contracts, stale heads, and accepted risks need separate labels.</li>
      <li><strong>Do not suppress an entire path to fix one mistake.</strong> Preserve the ability to find a different defect in the same code.</li>
      <li><strong>Do not call an inconclusive reviewer clean.</strong> Availability failure supplies no evidence that the code is correct.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      If repeated mistakes justify changing prompts, models, or policy, compare both
      disproved blockers and retained detection on representative changes. Our
      <a href="/blog/ai-code-review-benchmark">AI code review benchmark guide</a> covers
      that broader evaluation job. This tutorial deliberately starts smaller: settle
      one contested finding correctly before generalizing from it.
    </p>
    <p>
      ReviewGate supplies inspectable findings, a deterministic scoring contract, and
      structured state for that workflow. It does not edit code or merge for you. Start
      with the <a href="/docs/quickstart">GitHub Actions quickstart</a>, then use the
      <a href="/docs/agent-workflows">external-agent workflow</a> when you want an agent
      to act on current, still-open blockers. The goal is a defensible review decision,
      not an empty comment section.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="faq">
    <h2>Questions about AI code review false positives</h2>
    {faqs.map((faq) => <div><h3>{faq.question}</h3><p>{faq.answer}</p></div>)}
  </section>
</ArticleLayout>
